A C++ XRT host reaches the device dispatch floor, and 36 us is not a Python artifact
2026-09-09. env: mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2, llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201) + XRT SDK 2.21.75, native
Windows; driver 32.0.20101.3760; MSVC x64 (VS 2022 Build Tools), /std:c++17 /O2 /MD, linking
xrt_coreutil.lib. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), power mode Default (1.80 GHz per
results/aie/clock_probe_npu.log). No foreign hardware contexts: `xrt-smi examine -r
aie-partitions` reported "No hardware contexts running on device" before AND after every run,
and tools/host_load.ps1 reported HOST_LOAD_VERDICT CLEAR bracketing every series. Both
witnesses are printed by the driver script rather than asserted here.

Commands:
  scripts\build_dispatch_runner.bat
  ./scripts/run-dispatch-cpp.sh --iters 100 --batch-sizes 1,2,4,8,16,32,64      (twice)
  python3 kernels/dispatch_floor/measure_iron_batch.py --design passthrough \
      --payload 4096 --batch-sizes 1,8,64 --reps 3

WHY: results/aie/dispatch_runlist_npu.log reached 36.3 us per dispatch from raw pyxrt, and
results/aie/dispatch_iron_batch_npu.log showed IRON's own host path adds a near-constant ~500 us
per call that is FLAT IN BATCH SIZE. Both were measured through pybind11, so neither could say
how much of the remaining cost was the Python binding and how much was the driver -- and that
distinction decides whether a DEPLOYABLE runner can reach the device floor or whether ~500 us is
what a real caller always pays. dispatch_iron_batch_npu.log closed by naming this as the open
item: "reaching 36 us from a real design needs that host path written". This writes it in C++.

METHOD: kernels/dispatch_floor/dispatch_runner.cpp is a standalone C++ XRT host with no Python
anywhere in it. It loads the SAME compiled design the Python harness resolves out of
~/.npu/cache/ -- same final.xclbin, same insts.bin, same argument layout (opcode 3, instruction
BO, instruction word count, A, B, C) -- and drives it three ways: `single` (kernel()+wait, one at
a time), `rebuild` (a fresh xrt::runlist per iteration, which is exactly what measure_runlist.py
does, so the difference is the binding), and `persistent` (the runlist built ONCE and
execute()/wait() in a loop, which the Python harness never tried). scripts/run-dispatch-cpp.sh
runs the Python and C++ arms BACK TO BACK on the same cache entry in one sitting, because
CLAUDE.md's standing rule is that NPU latency on this machine drifts between sittings
independent of any code change -- the committed 2026-09-09 Python figures are not a valid
baseline for a C++ number measured on a different day.

The timed region is submit-to-completion only. One-time setup (device open, xclbin register,
hw_context, kernel, buffer allocation, instruction upload) is measured separately and excluded,
because the entire point of a cached-handle runner is that this is paid once: it read 33.1 ms in
run A and 60.3 ms in run B, i.e. it is large, variable, and irrelevant per-call after the first.

CORRECTNESS GATE: a run inside a runlist cannot be polled (xrt_kernel.h: run::state() "cannot be
called for run objects that are part of a runlist"), so runlist::wait() is the only completion
signal and VERIFYING OUTPUT BUFFERS IS THE ONLY CORRECTNESS GATE. Every row below verified all of
its output buffers against its inputs before its timing was kept, and the harness prints that
column. Every queued run owns its own buffer set; sharing them inside a runlist is undefined and
its symptom is wrong data rather than an error.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. A WRONG-DESIGN BUG THAT HAD TO BE FIXED FIRST, AND WHAT IT PRODUCED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The first attempt reported FAILED VERIFICATION on every batch size in BOTH arms, with the Python
arm's single dispatch reading 777.7 us against the ~140 us this design costs. The cause was not
the C++ host: measure_runlist.py resolved the wrong design out of ~/.npu/cache/.

Its rule was "the newest cache entry holding BOTH final.xclbin and insts.bin". Its own comment
claimed the insts.bin condition stopped "a GEMM, say" from being picked up -- but EVERY IRON
design writes insts.bin, so the extra condition excluded nothing at all. It was written when the
passthrough happened to be the newest thing in the cache. Once kernels/bank_placement/ and
kernels/gemm_reblock/ were compiled later the same day, "newest" was one of those, and the
harness timed a different design through the passthrough's argument layout.

That is a WRONG ANSWER, not an error, and it is worth recording because the same log that fixed
an earlier version of this same resolver had already warned about exactly this failure mode.
The two designs are not subtle: the passthrough is 75 instruction words, what it resolved was
1052.

FIXED by capturing the paths IRON itself resolved, rather than inferring them afterwards:
CompilableDesign.compile() returns (xclbin_path, inst_path) for the design being built and is
called on the first use of a specialization in a process -- on a cache HIT too, because compile()
is what consults the on-disk cache. measure_runlist.py now spies on it and REFUSES to fall back
to "newest" if the capture fails. Two other resolvers were tried first and are recorded in the
code so they are not re-attempted: CallableDesign.compilable.compile() recompiles (it is the
UNSPECIALIZED design, so it misses the cache and then dies on xclbinutil not being on PATH), and
importing the harness under a synthetic module name changes IRON's recipe hash and misses the
cache that way too.

Every number below this line was measured after the fix, with all arms verifying.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
