--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. WHAT THE NUMBERS SAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE 36 US FLOOR IS THE DRIVER'S, NOT PYTHON'S. This is the result. At N=64 the C++ host reads
36.7 us and pyxrt reads 35.9 us, in both series; from N=4 upward the two languages agree within
about 2%, and Python is marginally the FASTER of the two at every batch size >= 16. Removing
the binding entirely does not move the batched floor, so ~36 us is a property of the driver and
the device, and no host-side rewrite will go below it. That closes the question
dispatch_runlist_npu.log left open in the direction that makes its figure MORE trustworthy, not
less: 36.3 us was never a measurement of pybind overhead.

A DEPLOYABLE C++ RUNNER REACHES THAT FLOOR, WHICH IS THE OPEN ITEM CLOSED. Against the same
design in the same sitting, per dispatch: 671.5 us unbatched through IRON, 498.5 us batched
through IRON, 36.7 us through the C++ host. That is 18.3x better than IRON unbatched and 13.6x
better than IRON batched. IRON's ~460-573 us host share is not irreducible -- it is work a
cached-handle host does once at startup (33-60 ms here) instead of on every call.

THE SINGLE-DISPATCH PATH IS ONLY PARTLY PYTHON. C++ single runs 108.1/109.8 us mean against raw
pyxrt's 140.8/127.0, so roughly 20-30 us of the single-dispatch cost is binding overhead -- real,
but a minority. The C++ host still pays ~108 us for one dispatch against ~36.7 us batched, so
about 70 us per submission is driver-side work that ONLY batching amortizes, in any language.
Latency-critical single calls do not benefit from the rewrite; throughput does.

PERSISTENT RUNLISTS BUY ALMOST NOTHING, AND THAT IS ITSELF THE ANSWER TO A REASONABLE GUESS.
Building the runlist once and re-executing it beats rebuilding it only at N=1 (125.7 vs 148.5 us,
A) and N=2 (83.9 vs 95.6), and the two are indistinguishable from N=8 upward (47.4 vs 48.7;
36.7 vs 36.7 at N=64). So runlist CONSTRUCTION costs on the order of 20 us and is fully amortized
by N=8. The win in a C++ host comes from not being IRON, not from reusing the list. xrt::runlist
has no reset() in this SDK; re-execution works because execute() only refuses a list that is
currently executing.

THE GO/NO-GO THRESHOLD, STATED HONESTLY. docs/SILICON.md 3.4 makes every small-op verdict
conditional on this floor. There are now FOUR thresholds for the same design, not three, and
which one applies depends entirely on the host: 671.5 us (IRON, one call), 498.5 us (IRON,
batched 64), ~108 us (C++, one call), 36.7 us (C++ or pyxrt, batched 64). The 36 us figure is
reachable by a real deployable host -- but only for throughput workloads with >= 8 independent
dispatches in flight, and only outside IRON.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- One design: a 32 KB no-compute passthrough. A real kernel's compute plateaus above this floor
  (dispatch_iron_batch_npu.log measured bf16 GroupNorm plateauing at its own 838 us), so this
  bounds the HOST, not any model's latency.
- One payload (4096 int32) and one machine. No payload sweep was run in C++.
- The C++ host was not driven from a real model pipeline; it is a benchmark host, not an
  inference runtime. Nothing here shows that VitisAI EP or any ONNX path can use a runlist.
- Batching gives up per-call completion status entirely, so any deployment at 36 us inherits
  output verification as its only correctness gate.
- The one-time setup cost (33-60 ms) varied 1.8x between two runs of the identical binary and
  was not investigated.
- The C++ single-dispatch arm uses kernel()+wait() and so includes XRT's own run-object
  construction per call; a lower single-dispatch number may exist behind a pre-built run.
